theory.intro:
$y=\arcsin x$ and $y=\arctan x$ are **odd**: each has $180^{\circ}$ rotational symmetry about the origin. $y=\arccos x$ is **neither** odd nor even: for $-1\le x\le1$, $\arccos(-x)=\pi-\arccos x$, and neither parity identity holds throughout this domain.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.25]
\draw[->] (-1.9,0) -- (1.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.8) -- (0,3.9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,domain=0:3.1416,samples=80,smooth,variable=\t] plot ({cos(deg(\t))},{\t});
\draw[dashed] (-1.7,1.5708) -- (1.7,1.5708);
\fill (0.6,0.9273) circle (2pt);
\fill (-0.6,2.2143) circle (2pt);
\node[right,font=\small] at (0.66,0.9273) {$(a,\arccos a)$};
\node[left,font=\small] at (-0.66,2.2143) {$(-a,\pi-\arccos a)$};
\draw (1,0.07) -- (1,-0.07);
\node[below,font=\small] at (1,-0.07) {$1$};
\draw (-1,0.07) -- (-1,-0.07);
\node[below,font=\small] at (-1,-0.07) {$-1$};
\draw (0.09,1.5708) -- (-0.09,1.5708);
\node[below left,font=\small] at (-0.1,1.5708) {$\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\draw (0.09,3.1416) -- (-0.09,3.1416);
\node[left,font=\small] at (-0.12,3.1416) {$\pi$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

